---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SocialShare from "../../components/SocialShare.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="How to Create a QR Code: Step-by-Step Guide"
    description="Learn how to create a QR code for links, text, WiFi, and business cards. Step-by-step tutorial with logo options, bulk creation, and offline tips."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">
                Published: October 21, 2026 · Last Updated: October 24, 2026 ·
                By <a href="/about/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                    >Khuram Shahzad</a
                >
            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                How to Create a QR Code: Step-by-Step Guide
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                A QR code generator turns links, text, WiFi passwords and
                contact cards into a scannable square. Here's how to make one
                for free, add a logo, generate codes in bulk, and avoid the most
                common scanning mistakes.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                What Is a QR Code Generator?
            </h2>
            <p>
                A QR code generator is a tool that turns information into a
                scannable square pattern. That information can be a website
                link, plain text, a WiFi password, a contact card or a payment
                request. Anyone with a smartphone camera can then read it in a
                second.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>QR</strong> stands for <strong>Quick Response</strong>.
                Denso Wave, a Toyota supplier, created the format to track car
                parts on assembly lines. Because it stays readable when dirty or
                damaged, it spread far beyond factories.
            </p>
            <p>
                Most people searching for a QR code generator want one of these:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>A fast, free code for a link or text, with no signup</li>
                <li>A branded code with a logo, colors and a frame</li>
                <li>A code they can edit or track after printing</li>
                <li>Many codes at once from a spreadsheet</li>
                <li>A code made offline, for privacy or no connection</li>
                <li>A scanner or reader, to open a code they already have</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                This guide covers all six. Want to skip ahead and make one now?
                Try our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/qr-generator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    free QR code generator
                </a>.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                How to Create a QR Code in 4 Steps
            </h2>
            <p>The process is nearly identical on every generator:</p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Choose a content type.</strong> Pick URL, text, email,
                    SMS, WiFi, vCard or similar.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Enter your data.</strong> Paste the link or type the text.
                    Double-check it, because a typo gets baked into the code.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Customize (optional).</strong> Change colors, add a logo,
                    adjust the error correction level and the margin around the code.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Download and test.</strong> Export the file, then scan
                    it with at least two phones before you print or publish.
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                <strong>Pro tip:</strong> Always test with both an iPhone and an Android
                device. A code that scans on one but not the other usually has low
                contrast or an oversized logo.
            </p>
            <p>
                <a
                    href="/tools/qr-generator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    Generate your QR code now →
                </a>
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                QR Code Types You Can Generate
            </h2>
            <p>Free generators usually support the types below:</p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Type</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >What it does</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Best for</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Website URL</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Opens a webpage, form or video</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Flyers, packaging, posters</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Plain text</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Displays text in any language</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Notes, serial numbers, messages</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">vCard</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Saves a contact to the phone</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Business cards, networking</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Email</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Opens a pre-filled email</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Support, feedback, inquiries</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">SMS</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Opens a pre-written text message</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Promotions, opt-ins</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">WiFi</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Connects a device to a network</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Cafés, offices, guest homes</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Geo location</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Opens a map pin</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Event venues, store locations</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Calendar event</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Adds an event to the calendar</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Conferences, webinars</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Payment / crypto</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Requests a payment</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Donations, small merchants</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-4">
                Platforms that sell paid plans often advertise 20 or more types,
                including PDFs, menus, app-store links and landing pages. Most
                of those are really URL codes pointing at a hosted page.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>What can a QR code hold?</strong> Static codes store the data
                directly in the pattern. The practical ceiling is around 3 KB of binary
                data, and that is why a short link beats a long block of text.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Static vs. Dynamic QR Codes
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is the single most important decision you'll make, and many
                guides gloss over it.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Static QR codes</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>The data is written into the pattern itself</li>
                <li>
                    They never expire and don't depend on any company's servers
                </li>
                <li>They cannot be edited after creation</li>
                <li>They can't track scans</li>
                <li>They are usually free</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Dynamic QR codes</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    The code contains a short redirect link, and the redirect
                    points to your real destination
                </li>
                <li>You can change the destination after printing</li>
                <li>
                    They usually offer scan analytics (location, device, time)
                </li>
                <li>
                    They typically require a paid plan, and if you stop paying,
                    the redirect may stop working
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                <strong>Key insight:</strong> You cannot convert a static code into
                a dynamic one later. If you might need to fix a typo, change a campaign
                page or measure results, decide before you print.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                A warning about "free" codes that expire
            </h3>
            <p>
                Some providers quietly generate a dynamic code, even when you
                thought you made a static one. The code works for a trial
                period, then stops until you pay. Before printing thousands of
                copies, scan your test code and check the URL it opens. If it
                passes through the provider's own domain instead of your site,
                it's a redirect.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong></p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Permanent info (WiFi, a contact, a fixed website): use
                    static
                </li>
                <li>
                    Marketing, menus, anything that might change or needs
                    analytics: use dynamic
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Free QR Code Generator: What "Free" Really Means
            </h2>
            <p>"Free" varies a lot. Here's what to check:</p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Question</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Why it matters</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Is it truly static?</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Static codes can't be switched off</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Is commercial use allowed?</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Matters for packaging, ads and resale</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Are print-quality formats free?</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >You want SVG or EPS for large prints, not just
                                a small PNG</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Is signup required?</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Some tools gate downloads behind an account</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Is your data stored or tracked?</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >Matters for privacy, especially for vCards and
                                WiFi</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-4">
                One well-known free tool lets you download vector formats (SVG,
                EPS) without registration and permits commercial use. Others
                allow only a low-resolution image without an account, and
                reserve PNG, SVG or EPS for signed-in users. So "free" can mean
                very different things in practice.
            </p>
            <p>
                The QR code technology itself is free to use. The patent holder
                chose not to enforce its rights on the ISO-standardized format,
                so no license fee applies. The name "QR Code" is a registered
                trademark, though.
            </p>
            <p><strong>What to look for in a free generator:</strong></p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>No forced signup for basic codes</li>
                <li>No watermark</li>
                <li>Vector downloads (SVG) for print</li>
                <li>Adjustable error correction</li>
                <li>A clear statement that codes don't expire</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Use our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/qr-generator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    free QR generator
                </a>, with no signup needed →
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                QR Code Generator With Logo and Custom Design
            </h2>
            <p>
                A plain black-and-white code works, but a branded one tells
                people what to expect and tends to get scanned more. One
                platform's marketing claims its branded codes get 3.8 times more
                scans than plain ones. That's a vendor figure rather than
                independent research, so treat it as directional. A separate
                analysis of one QR platform's data found a custom-designed code
                out-scanned a plain one by 2.4x at the median in consumer
                packaged goods.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                How logos work in a QR code
            </h3>
            <p>
                QR codes use Reed-Solomon error correction, the same math that
                lets scratched audio CDs still play. Even if part of the code is
                hidden or damaged, the scanner can rebuild the missing data.
            </p>
            <p>
                A logo placed in the center deliberately "damages" part of the
                code. It works because error correction fills in the gap.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                The four error correction levels
            </h3>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Level</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Approximate recovery</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >When to use</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">L (Low)</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">~7%</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Clean digital screens, maximum data capacity</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">M (Medium)</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">~15%</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">General use</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Q (Quartile)</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">~25%</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Codes with small logos</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">H (High)</td>
                            <td class="p-3">~30%</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >Codes with logos, rough surfaces, outdoor use</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-4">
                Higher correction means more modules, so the code looks denser.
                For a logo, choose Q or H.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Logo and color rules that keep codes scannable
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Keep the logo small. Roughly 15 to 20% of the code area at
                    most.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Use strong contrast. The dark modules must be clearly darker
                    than the background.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Never invert the colors. A light pattern on a dark
                    background (a "negative") confuses many readers.
                </li>
                <li>Avoid busy backgrounds behind the code.</li>
                <li>
                    Leave the quiet zone. The blank margin around the code is
                    part of the code.
                </li>
                <li>Don't distort it. Keep the aspect ratio square.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Always scan-test after styling. Artistic "design" codes look
                striking but carry more risk, so test them on several devices.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                QR Code Generator for Text
            </h2>
            <p>
                A text QR code shows a block of text when scanned, with no
                internet needed. That makes it useful for:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Short instructions or labels</li>
                <li>Serial numbers and inventory IDs</li>
                <li>Multilingual notes (text codes support any language)</li>
                <li>Offline scavenger hunts and classroom activities</li>
                <li>Product care notes or warranty codes</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Keep it short.</strong> Some older scanner apps struggle with
                text longer than about 300 characters, and every extra character makes
                the code denser and harder to scan. For anything longer, put the text
                on a page and encode the link instead.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Special characters:</strong> If accented letters or symbols
                appear garbled, the cause is usually character encoding. Choose a
                generator that encodes in UTF-8.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Google, Canva and Other Ways to Generate a QR Code
            </h2>
            <p>You don't always need a dedicated website.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                QR code generator with Google (Chrome)
            </h3>
            <p>
                Chrome has a built-in option. Open the page you want to share,
                then use the share menu in the address bar and choose the QR
                code option. It produces a code for the current page that you
                can download. It's ideal for sending a page from desktop to
                phone, but it's limited to the page you're viewing, with minimal
                styling.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                QR code generator with Canva
            </h3>
            <p>
                Canva offers QR code creation inside its design editor, so you
                can drop a code directly onto a flyer, poster or presentation.
                Its strength is layout: you can design around the code without
                exporting and re-importing. Its limit is that it's built for
                design, not analytics, so you won't get scan tracking from it.
                Check Canva's current options in the editor, as features change.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Other options</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Phone sharing menus. Many phones can show a QR code for a
                    link, contact or WiFi network.
                </li>
                <li>Spreadsheet add-ons and plugins for lists of codes.</li>
                <li>
                    Command-line and code libraries for developers (see the next
                    section).
                </li>
                <li>
                    Browser extensions that create a code for the current tab.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Method</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Best for</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Main limit</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Web generator</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Fast, custom codes</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Varies by tool</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Chrome built-in</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Sharing the current page</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Little customization</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Canva</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Designed printables</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">No scan analytics</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Extensions</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">One-click page codes</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Limited styling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Code libraries</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Automation, privacy</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Requires technical skill</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Offline QR Code Generator
            </h2>
            <p>
                Offline generation means the code is created on your own device,
                and your data never touches someone else's server. Reasons to do
                it:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Privacy for WiFi passwords, contact details or internal data
                </li>
                <li>No internet in the field or on a restricted network</li>
                <li>Automation for hundreds of codes without rate limits</li>
                <li>Permanence. Nothing depends on a third-party service</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Option 1: Desktop software
            </h3>
            <p>
                Several free desktop QR programs work without a connection.
                Download from the official source only.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Option 2: A short Python script
            </h3>
            <p>
                If you're comfortable with a terminal, this takes under a
                minute:
            </p>
            <pre
                class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code># pip install "qrcode[pil]"
import qrcode

img = qrcode.make("https://example.com")
img.save("my-qr.png")</code></pre>
            <p>For a logo-friendly code, set high error correction:</p>
            <pre
                class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>import qrcode
from qrcode.constants import ERROR_CORRECT_H

qr = qrcode.QRCode(error_correction=ERROR_CORRECT_H, box_size=10, border=4)
qr.add_data("https://example.com")
qr.make(fit=True)
qr.make_image(fill_color="black", back_color="white").save("my-qr-h.png")</code></pre>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Option 3: A browser tool that runs locally
            </h3>
            <p>
                Some web generators run entirely in your browser. If privacy
                matters, check the privacy policy or confirm the tool still
                works with your network disconnected after the page loads.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Reminder:</strong> Offline codes are static by nature. They
                have no tracking, but they also never expire.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Bulk QR Code Generator</h2>
            <p>
                A bulk generator creates many unique codes at once, typically
                from a spreadsheet. Common uses:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Inventory and asset tags (one code per item)</li>
                <li>Event tickets and badges (one code per attendee)</li>
                <li>Product labels with batch or serial numbers</li>
                <li>Table-specific menus in restaurants</li>
                <li>Student or employee ID cards</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                The standard workflow
            </h3>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Prepare a spreadsheet. One column for the data (URL or ID),
                    one for the filename.
                </li>
                <li>Upload or paste it into a bulk-capable tool.</li>
                <li>
                    Choose the shared design (size, colors, logo, error
                    correction).
                </li>
                <li>Generate and download as a ZIP of image files.</li>
                <li>Spot-check a sample before printing the full run.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Script it yourself</h3>
            <pre
                class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>import csv, qrcode

with open("codes.csv", newline="") as f:
    for row in csv.DictReader(f):
        qrcode.make(row["url"]).save(f"&#123;row['name']&#125;.png")</code></pre>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Bulk best practices</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Use unique, trackable identifiers in each URL, such as
                    ?id=1042
                </li>
                <li>
                    Name files predictably so printing and matching are easy
                </li>
                <li>
                    Choose static or dynamic deliberately. Dynamic bulk codes
                    let you update thousands of destinations later, but they
                    usually need a paid plan
                </li>
                <li>
                    Test a random sample from the batch, not just the first code
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                QR Code Scanner and Reader: How to Scan Any Code
            </h2>
            <p>
                People searching "qr scanner" or "qr code reader" often already
                have a code and want to open it.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Scan on iPhone</h3>
            <p>
                Open the Camera app, point it at the code, and tap the banner
                that appears. No extra app needed. See our full guide on{" "}
                <a
                    href="/blog/scan-qr-code-iphone/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    how to scan QR codes on iPhone
                </a>.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Scan on Android</h3>
            <p>
                Open the Camera app and point it at the code. If nothing
                appears, open Google Lens or the QR scanner option in Quick
                Settings, depending on your device. See our full guide on{" "}
                <a
                    href="/blog/scan-qr-code-android/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    how to scan QR codes on Android
                </a>.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Scan from an image or screenshot
            </h3>
            <p>
                Open the image in your photo app and use the built-in text and
                code recognition, or use Google Lens. On a computer, use a
                reader tool that accepts an uploaded image.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Scan on a computer</h3>
            <p>
                Desktop browsers can't aim a camera at paper easily, so upload
                the image to an online reader or use a browser extension.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Scanner tips</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Hold your phone steady, 10 to 30 cm away</li>
                <li>Make sure the code is well lit with no glare</li>
                <li>Fill the frame, but keep all four corners visible</li>
                <li>Clean the camera lens</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Always read the preview.</strong> Your phone shows the destination
                before opening it. Check it first (see the security section below).
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Design and Printing Best Practices
            </h2>
            <p>
                A code that looks great but fails in the real world is worse
                than a plain one.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Size</h3>
            <p>
                A common guideline is a minimum edge length of about 2 cm (0.8
                in) for most uses. Some sources allow down to about 1.5 cm (0.6
                in) for very simple codes, but smaller is riskier. As a rule,
                scan distance divided by 10 gives a good minimum size. A code
                scanned from 1 meter should be at least 10 cm wide.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Quiet zone</h3>
            <p>
                Leave a blank margin of at least 4 modules (the little squares)
                on every side. Crowding the edge is a top cause of failed scans.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Contrast and color</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Dark pattern on light background</li>
                <li>Avoid pale yellow, light gray or neon on white</li>
                <li>Don't invert</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">File format</h3>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Format</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Use</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">SVG / EPS</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Print, signage, packaging (vector, scales
                                perfectly)</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">PNG</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Web, social, documents</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">JPG</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >Quick sharing (avoid for fine detail)</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Call to action</h3>
            <p>
                A bare code gets ignored. Add a short prompt like "Scan for the
                menu" or "Scan to connect". Frames and labels measurably help,
                because people scan when they know why.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">Placement</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Put the code where people can reach it and hold still. Not
                    on a moving vehicle, a high billboard or a curved bottle
                    neck.
                </li>
                <li>Avoid glossy finishes that cause glare</li>
                <li>
                    Make sure there's mobile signal where it's scanned, if the
                    code opens a website
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                QR Code Security: Avoiding "Quishing"
            </h2>
            <p>
                QR phishing ("quishing") hides malicious links in codes.
                Microsoft recorded a 146% rise in QR code phishing in the first
                quarter of 2026.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Safe-scanning checklist
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Read the URL preview before tapping. Look for misspelled
                    domains
                </li>
                <li>
                    Be suspicious of codes stuck over other codes (a common
                    tampering trick)
                </li>
                <li>
                    Don't enter passwords or payment details after scanning an
                    unexpected code
                </li>
                <li>
                    Avoid codes in unsolicited emails or letters, especially
                    urgent ones
                </li>
                <li>
                    Use official apps for parking, banking and payments where
                    possible
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                If you publish codes
            </h3>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Use a domain you control</li>
                <li>Prefer HTTPS links</li>
                <li>
                    Check regularly that the code still points where you expect
                </li>
                <li>
                    On printed items in public spaces, consider tamper-resistant
                    placement
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">QR Code Statistics</h2>
            <p>
                Usage keeps climbing, though numbers vary by source and
                methodology.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    70% of US consumers scan a QR code at least monthly,
                    according to Uniqode's 2026 research
                </li>
                <li>
                    eMarketer projects 102.6 million US smartphone users will
                    scan QR codes in 2026
                </li>
                <li>
                    94% of marketers increased QR code usage in the past year,
                    per Bitly's 2025 survey
                </li>
                <li>
                    On one platform's 69.9 million recorded scans, the top 5% of
                    codes drove 78 to 95% of all scans
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                That last figure is the most useful for creators. Most codes get
                very few scans, and a small share does almost all the work. The
                difference usually comes down to placement, a clear call to
                action, and a destination worth visiting.
            </p>
            <p>
                Note that vendor statistics often reflect the vendor's own
                customers, so read them as indicators rather than universal
                truths.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Troubleshooting: Why Your QR Code Won't Scan
            </h2>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Problem</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Likely cause</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Fix</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Won't scan at all</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Low contrast or inverted colors</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Use dark on light</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Scans only up close</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Code too small or too dense</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Enlarge; shorten the data</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Works on one phone only</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Logo too big</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Reduce logo; raise error correction</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Garbled text</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Encoding issue</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Use UTF-8; shorten text</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Opens wrong page</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Typo in the URL</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Regenerate (static codes can't be edited)</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Stopped working later</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Expired dynamic redirect</td
                            >
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Renew plan or switch to static</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Blurry print</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Low-resolution image</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >Re-export as SVG or high-res PNG</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Fails on curved surface</td>
                            <td class="p-3">Distortion</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >Flatten the area or enlarge the code</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                How to Choose the Right QR Generator
            </h2>
            <p>Match the tool to the job:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>One-off link, text or WiFi:</strong> a free static generator
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Print marketing with a logo:</strong> a static generator
                    with vector export, or a design tool
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Campaigns you'll measure or edit:</strong> a dynamic platform,
                    understanding the ongoing cost
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Hundreds of codes:</strong> a bulk tool or a script
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Sensitive data:</strong> an offline or local tool
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Designed collateral:</strong> a design platform with a
                    built-in QR option
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                If a tool won't tell you whether its codes expire, assume they
                might.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What is the best free QR code generator?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        The best one depends on your needs. For simple static
                        codes, choose a tool that has no signup, no watermark,
                        allows commercial use, and offers SVG downloads. Our{
                            " "
                        }
                        <a
                            href="/tools/qr-generator/"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >QR generator</a
                        >{" "}
                        is built around that checklist.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Are QR codes free to make?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes, static codes are free to create and use, including
                        commercially, on most tools. Costs typically apply only
                        to dynamic codes with editing and analytics.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Do QR codes expire?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        A true static code never expires, because the data lives
                        in the pattern. Dynamic codes depend on a redirect
                        service and can stop working if the plan lapses.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I edit a QR code after printing?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Only if it's dynamic. A static code can't be changed, so
                        a typo means reprinting.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        How do I make a QR code with a logo?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Use a generator with a logo option, set error correction
                        to Q or H, keep the logo small, and scan-test the result
                        on multiple phones.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        How do I scan a QR code?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Open your phone's camera, point it at the code, and tap
                        the link that appears. Android users can also use Google
                        Lens. For a full walkthrough, see our guides on{" "}
                        <a
                            href="/blog/scan-qr-code-iphone/"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >scanning QR codes on iPhone</a
                        >{" "}
                        and{" "}
                        <a
                            href="/blog/scan-qr-code-android/"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >scanning QR codes on Android</a
                        >.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Conclusion</h2>
            <p>
                A QR code generator is one of the fastest ways to connect
                printed or physical things to digital content. The right choice
                comes down to a few decisions:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Static or dynamic?</strong> Static for permanence, dynamic
                    for edits and tracking.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>How much design?</strong> Use a logo and color, but keep
                    contrast high and error correction at Q or H.
                </li>
                <li><strong>How many?</strong> One at a time, or in bulk.</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Where will it live?</strong> Print needs vector files
                    and generous size.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Who's scanning?</strong> Make the destination mobile-friendly,
                    and give people a reason to scan.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>Above all, test before you print.</p>
            <p>
                Ready to make yours?{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/qr-generator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    Create a free QR code now →
                </a>{" "}
                It takes seconds, and you can download it for print or web.
            </p>
